<h1>Adam Adamandy Kochański</h1>
<p>Biography here is a secondary layer, not the spine. The site tells the story of a problem he worked on, not the order of his moves. Below is only what is needed to place the instruments in time — with the points where the scholarship disagrees marked openly.</p>
<h2>Timeline</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>1631</strong> — born in the Dobrzyń region. <em>One museum text gives 1637; 1631 is the prevailing version.</em></li>
<li><strong>1652</strong> — Jesuit novitiate in Vilnius, then studies at the {% objasnij "Vilnius Academy", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vilnius_University", "The Jesuit Academy and University of Vilnius, today Vilnius University" %}.</li>
<li><strong>1660s–1670s</strong> — teaching at European colleges: Florence, Prague, Olomouc, Würzburg, Mainz.</li>
<li><strong>1659</strong> — the idea of a magnetic clock {% objasnij "pendulum", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pendulum_clock", "The clock pendulum: a weight on a suspension whose period of swing measures time" %}, devised at Mainz. The author gave the date himself, in an account published twenty-six years later: {% zrodlo "acta-1685-perpendiculum", "<em>Acta Eruditorum</em> 1685" %}.</li>
<li><strong>1664</strong> — <em class="dymek-w-tekscie" data-dymek="Some of the literature gives this title as “Mirabilia chronometrica”. The site keeps the wording of the printed book: “Mirabilis chronometrica”.">Mirabilis chronometrica</em>, book IX of {% objasnij "Caspar Schott", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaspar_Schott", "Kaspar Schott, German Jesuit and author of technical compendia" %}'s {% zrodlo "technica-curiosa", "<em>Technica curiosa</em>" %}.</li>
<li><strong>1667</strong> — the magnetic pendulum made at Florence and shown to Ferdinand II, Grand Duke of Tuscany. The print has <em lang="la">spectandum exhibui</em> — he put it up to be seen.</li>
<li><strong>c. 1672</strong> — a {% objasnij "spring", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balance_spring", "The hairspring: a flat spiral spring at the balance, part of the regulator" %} regulating the {% objasnij "balance wheel", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balance_wheel", "The balance wheel: an oscillating wheel that keeps time in a watch in place of a pendulum" %} in pocket watches. The author dates this observation separately from the idea of 1659: {% zrodlo "acta-1685-perpendiculum", "<em>Acta Eruditorum</em> 1685" %}.</li>
<li><strong>1678, 1679 or 1680</strong> — arrival at the court of {% objasnij "John III Sobieski", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_III_Sobieski", "John III Sobieski, King of Poland 1674–1696" %}. <em>Three different dates appear in the literature.</em></li>
</ul>
{% pytanie "A5", jezyk %}
<ul>
<li><strong>c. 1684</strong> — the sundial on the garden front of {% objasnij "the palace at Wilanów", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilan%C3%B3w_Palace", "Wilanów Palace, John III Sobieski's residence outside Warsaw" %}, in correspondence with {% objasnij "Hevelius", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Hevelius", "Johannes Hevelius, astronomer of Gdańsk" %}.</li>
<li><strong>1685</strong> — <em>Observationes cyclometricae</em>, {% zrodlo "acta-1685", "<em>Acta Eruditorum</em>" %}.</li>
<li><strong>1691–1700</strong> — correspondence with {% objasnij "Leibniz", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gottfried_Wilhelm_Leibniz", "Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz, philosopher and mathematician" %} resumed after a gap of some twenty years.</li>
<li><strong>1700</strong> — died on 17 May at {% objasnij "Teplice", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teplice", "Teplice in northern Bohemia, a spa town" %} in Bohemia. <span class="dymek-w-tekscie" data-dymek="This is Teplice in Bohemia, not Cieplice Śląskie-Zdrój near Jelenia Góra in Poland, with which it is sometimes confused.">Not to be confused with Cieplice near Jelenia Góra.</span></li>
</ul>
{% pytanie "A10", jezyk %}

<h2>What this timeline leaves out</h2>
<p>It does not say that Kochański devoted much of his last years to the search for {% objasnij "perpetual motion", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perpetual_motion", "A machine that would run without any supply of energy from outside" %}. It does not say that the magnetic-{% objasnij "balance", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balance_wheel", "The balance wheel: an oscillating wheel that keeps time in a watch in place of a pendulum" %} watch was a dead end and that the {% objasnij "marine clock", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_chronometer", "The marine chronometer: a clock accurate enough to give longitude after weeks at sea" %} was never built. The failures belong to this biography and are listed here deliberately — a site without them would be hagiography, not an account of work.</p>
